(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["interpretation-of-medical-literature-book-questions-chapter-01-introduction-quiz"]={"title":"Chapter 1: Introduction Quiz — Book Questions — Interpretation of Medical Literature","category":"Interpretation of Medical Literature","exam":"Book Questions","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 37-year-old woman with low back pain for the past 4 weeks wants to know if you recommend surgery. You prefer to base your treatment recommendations on research evidence whenever possible. In the strongest study you can find, investigators reviewed the medical records of 40 consecutive men with low back pain under care at their clinic—22 had been referred for surgery and the other 18 patients had remained under medical care without surgery. The study compared rates of disabling pain after 2 months. All of the surgically treated patients and 10 of the medically treated patients were still being seen in the clinic throughout this time. Rates of pain relief were slightly higher in the surgically treated patients.\n\nBecause there are relatively few patients in this study, it may give a misleading impression of the actual effectiveness of surgery.\n\nWhich response best represents the corresponding threat to validity?","choices":["Selection bias","Measurement bias","Confounding","Chance","External validity (generalizability)"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Chance is random variation producing conclusions that differ from the true results, and a small sample can give a misleading impression of the population it came from. Statistical analysis can estimate chance, and good design reduces it, but it is never totally eliminated. — 1 PAJ5512 Introduction Bias Validity MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 47; 2 PAJ5512 Study Design and Research Methods MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 30; Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 1.1"},{"q":"A 37-year-old woman with low back pain for the past 4 weeks wants to know if you recommend surgery. You prefer to base your treatment recommendations on research evidence whenever possible. In the strongest study you can find, investigators reviewed the medical records of 40 consecutive men with low back pain under care at their clinic—22 had been referred for surgery and the other 18 patients had remained under medical care without surgery. The study compared rates of disabling pain after 2 months. All of the surgically treated patients and 10 of the medically treated patients were still being seen in the clinic throughout this time. Rates of pain relief were slightly higher in the surgically treated patients.\n\nThe results of this study may not apply to your patient, a woman, because all the patients in the study were men.\n\nWhich response best represents the corresponding threat to validity?","choices":["Selection bias","Measurement bias","Confounding","Chance","External validity (generalizability)"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. External validity, also called generalizability, is the degree to which results are also true in other clinical settings and patients. Applying a study of men to a woman assumes surgery works the same way in both sexes, and this is rarely settled by a single study. — 1 PAJ5512 Introduction Bias Validity MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 50; 1 PAJ5512 Introduction Bias Validity MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 51; Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 1.2"},{"q":"A 37-year-old woman with low back pain for the past 4 weeks wants to know if you recommend surgery. You prefer to base your treatment recommendations on research evidence whenever possible. In the strongest study you can find, investigators reviewed the medical records of 40 consecutive men with low back pain under care at their clinic—22 had been referred for surgery and the other 18 patients had remained under medical care without surgery. The study compared rates of disabling pain after 2 months. All of the surgically treated patients and 10 of the medically treated patients were still being seen in the clinic throughout this time. Rates of pain relief were slightly higher in the surgically treated patients.\n\nFewer patients who did not have surgery remained under care at the clinic 2 months after surgery.\n\nWhich response best represents the corresponding threat to validity?","choices":["Selection bias","Measurement bias","Confounding","Chance","External validity (generalizability)"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Measurement bias means participants in one group are more likely than another to have their outcome detected. All the surgically treated patients but only 10 of 18 medically treated patients stayed under care, so pain was not measured with equal completeness in the two groups. — 7 PAJ5512 Prognosis MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 24; 1 PAJ5512 Introduction Bias Validity MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 41; Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 1.3"},{"q":"A 37-year-old woman with low back pain for the past 4 weeks wants to know if you recommend surgery. You prefer to base your treatment recommendations on research evidence whenever possible. In the strongest study you can find, investigators reviewed the medical records of 40 consecutive men with low back pain under care at their clinic—22 had been referred for surgery and the other 18 patients had remained under medical care without surgery. The study compared rates of disabling pain after 2 months. All of the surgically treated patients and 10 of the medically treated patients were still being seen in the clinic throughout this time. Rates of pain relief were slightly higher in the surgically treated patients.\n\nThe patients who were referred for surgery were younger and fitter than those who remained under medical care.\n\nWhich response best represents the corresponding threat to validity?","choices":["Selection bias","Measurement bias","Confounding","Chance","External validity (generalizability)"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Selection bias occurs when compared groups differ in more than the focus of the study. Patients referred for surgery were younger and fitter, so better recovery may reflect who was chosen for surgery rather than what surgery did. — 1 PAJ5512 Introduction Bias Validity MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 40; Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 1.4"},{"q":"A 37-year-old woman with low back pain for the past 4 weeks wants to know if you recommend surgery. You prefer to base your treatment recommendations on research evidence whenever possible. In the strongest study you can find, investigators reviewed the medical records of 40 consecutive men with low back pain under care at their clinic—22 had been referred for surgery and the other 18 patients had remained under medical care without surgery. The study compared rates of disabling pain after 2 months. All of the surgically treated patients and 10 of the medically treated patients were still being seen in the clinic throughout this time. Rates of pain relief were slightly higher in the surgically treated patients.\n\nCompared with patients who had medical care alone, patients who had surgery might have been less likely to report whatever pain they had and the treating physicians might have been less inclined to record pain in the medical record.\n\nWhich response best represents the corresponding threat to validity?","choices":["Selection bias","Measurement bias","Confounding","Chance","External validity (generalizability)"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Measurement bias means the method of measuring leads to incorrect results, including when one group is less likely to have its outcome detected. If surgical patients reported less pain and physicians recorded less pain for them, the outcome was measured unequally between the groups. — 1 PAJ5512 Introduction Bias Validity MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 41; 7 PAJ5512 Prognosis MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 24; Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 1.5"},{"q":"A 37-year-old woman with low back pain for the past 4 weeks wants to know if you recommend surgery. You prefer to base your treatment recommendations on research evidence whenever possible. In the strongest study you can find, investigators reviewed the medical records of 40 consecutive men with low back pain under care at their clinic—22 had been referred for surgery and the other 18 patients had remained under medical care without surgery. The study compared rates of disabling pain after 2 months. All of the surgically treated patients and 10 of the medically treated patients were still being seen in the clinic throughout this time. Rates of pain relief were slightly higher in the surgically treated patients.\n\nPatients without other medical conditions were both more likely to recover and more likely to be referred for surgery.\n\nWhich response best represents the corresponding threat to validity?","choices":["Selection bias","Measurement bias","Confounding","Chance","External validity (generalizability)"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Confounding occurs when a covariate is associated with both the exposure and the outcome, so its effect cannot be separated from the effect of the treatment. Having no other medical conditions makes patients more likely to be referred for surgery and more likely to recover, and it could explain the difference by itself. — 1 PAJ5512 Introduction Bias Validity MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 42; Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 1.6"},{"q":"Histamine is a mediator of inflammation in patients with allergic rhinitis (“hay fever”). Based on this fact, which of the following is true?","choices":["Drugs that block the effects of histamines will relieve symptoms.","A fall in histamine levels in the nose is a reliable marker of clinical success.","Antihistamines may be effective, and their effects on symptoms (e.g., itchy nose, sneezing, and congestion) should be studied in patients with allergic rhinitis.","Other mediators are not important.","If laboratory studies of disease are convincing, clinical research is unnecessary."],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The fact that histamine mediates inflammation leads to a promising hypothesis that antihistamines may work, but it must be tested. Their effects on symptoms such as itchy nose, sneezing and congestion should be studied in patients with allergic rhinitis. — Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 1.7"},{"q":"Which of the following statements about samples of populations is incorrect?","choices":["Samples of populations may have characteristics that differ from the population even though correct sampling procedures were followed.","Samples of populations are the only feasible way of studying the population.","When populations are correctly sampled, external validity is ensured.","Samples of populations should be selected in a way that every member of the population has an equal chance of being chosen."],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: this is the incorrect statement. Sampling a population correctly does not ensure external validity, because samples can misrepresent the population by chance, especially small ones. External validity, whether results hold in other clinical settings, is rarely determined from a single study. — 1 PAJ5512 Introduction Bias Validity MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 47; 1 PAJ5512 Introduction Bias Validity MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 51; 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 29; 8 PAJ5512 Research and Trials MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 14; Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 1.8"},{"q":"Because reports suggested estrogens increase the risk of clotting, a study compared the frequency of oral contraceptive use among women admitted to a hospital with thrombophlebitis and a group of women admitted for other reasons. Medical records were reviewed for indication of oral contraceptive use in the two groups. Women with thrombophlebitis were found to have been using oral contraceptives more frequently than the women admitted for other reasons.\n\nWomen with thrombophlebitis may have reported the use of contraceptives more completely than women without thrombophlebitis because they remembered hearing of the association.\n\nWhich response best represents the corresponding threat to validity?","choices":["Selection bias","Measurement bias","Confounding","Chance","External validity (generalizability)"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Asking people about a past exposure relies on memory, and recall bias occurs when those with the illness recall exposures they think are related to it more completely. That makes the exposure measured unequally in the two groups, so it is measurement bias. — 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 33; 1 PAJ5512 Introduction Bias Validity MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 41; Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 1.12"},{"q":"Because reports suggested estrogens increase the risk of clotting, a study compared the frequency of oral contraceptive use among women admitted to a hospital with thrombophlebitis and a group of women admitted for other reasons. Medical records were reviewed for indication of oral contraceptive use in the two groups. Women with thrombophlebitis were found to have been using oral contraceptives more frequently than the women admitted for other reasons.\n\nDoctors may have questioned women with thrombophlebitis more carefully about contraceptive use than they did those without thrombophlebitis (and recorded the information more carefully in the medical record) because they were aware that estrogen could cause clotting.\n\nWhich response best represents the corresponding threat to validity?","choices":["Selection bias","Measurement bias","Confounding","Chance","External validity (generalizability)"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Measurement bias occurs when information is not obtained the same way in both groups. Clinicians who knew estrogen can cause clotting questioned and recorded contraceptive use more carefully in women with thrombophlebitis, so exposure was measured unequally. — 7 PAJ5512 Prognosis MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 24; 1 PAJ5512 Introduction Bias Validity MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 41; Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 1.13"},{"q":"Because reports suggested estrogens increase the risk of clotting, a study compared the frequency of oral contraceptive use among women admitted to a hospital with thrombophlebitis and a group of women admitted for other reasons. Medical records were reviewed for indication of oral contraceptive use in the two groups. Women with thrombophlebitis were found to have been using oral contraceptives more frequently than the women admitted for other reasons.\n\nThe number of women in the study was small.\n\nWhich response best represents the corresponding threat to validity?","choices":["Selection bias","Measurement bias","Confounding","Chance","External validity (generalizability)"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Chance is random variation producing conclusions that differ from the true results, and small numbers of participants increase the possibility that chance accounts for a difference between groups. Statistical analysis can estimate the role of chance. — 1 PAJ5512 Introduction Bias Validity MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 47; 2 PAJ5512 Study Design and Research Methods MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 30; Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 1.14"},{"q":"Because reports suggested estrogens increase the risk of clotting, a study compared the frequency of oral contraceptive use among women admitted to a hospital with thrombophlebitis and a group of women admitted for other reasons. Medical records were reviewed for indication of oral contraceptive use in the two groups. Women with thrombophlebitis were found to have been using oral contraceptives more frequently than the women admitted for other reasons.\n\nThe women with thrombophlebitis were admitted to the hospital by doctors working in different neighborhoods than the physicians of those that did not have thrombophlebitis.\n\nWhich response best represents the corresponding threat to validity?","choices":["Selection bias","Measurement bias","Confounding","Chance","External validity (generalizability)"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Selection bias occurs when compared groups differ in more than the focus of the study. Women admitted by doctors in different neighborhoods likely differ in socioeconomic and other characteristics related to health outcomes, so the groups were not comparable to begin with. — 1 PAJ5512 Introduction Bias Validity MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 40; Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 1.15"}]};
